Embedding is declaring a field with no explicit name — the type name becomes it:

```go
type User struct{ Name string; Age int }
func (u User) Greet() string { return "hi " + u.Name }

type Admin struct {
    User       // embedded — field name IS 'User'
    Level int
}

a := Admin{User: User{Name: "Root", Age: 40}, Level: 9}
fmt.Println(a.Name, a.Greet(), a.Level)  // Root hi Root 9 — promoted
```

Executed. Both the field (Name) and the method (Greet) arrive on Admin. That promotion is the feature: the embedded type's exported fields and methods become part of the outer type's surface, reachable without naming the embedded type — unless shadowed by an outer declaration.

What embedding provides, precisely: reuse (Admin carries User's implementation), promotion (unqualified access), interface satisfaction through promotion (if User satisfies Greeter, Admin does too — the embedded methods count in Admin's method set), and delegation wiring (embed an interface, and the struct satisfies it while forwarding to the concrete value stored in the field).

What it is NOT, and the distinction is the interview's core: inheritance. No subtype (Admin is not a User — no assignment, no polymorphism), no overriding (an outer Greet SHADOWS; User's remains via a.User.Greet()), no dynamic dispatch (method calls bind to the receiver type statically). Embedding is composition with sugar — the sugar being that the inner's surface appears on the outer.

Interface-in-struct embedding is the delegation pattern:

```go
type LogReader struct {
    io.Reader            // embedded INTERFACE
    prefix string
}

func (l LogReader) Read(p []byte) (int, error) {
    n, err := l.Reader.Read(p)  // or: rely on promotion below
    // wrap/add behavior...
    return n, err
}
```

Two effects, both structural:

1. The struct SATISFIES the interface — io.Reader's method set (Read) counts as part of LogReader's method set via promotion. The compiler verifies it exactly as if you'd declared a Read method: var _ io.Reader = LogReader{}.

2. The promoted method FORWARDS to the field's value: l.Read(p) calls the io.Reader stored in l — whatever concrete reader was assigned at construction. The struct delegates by default.

The pattern this composes into — selective overriding:

```go
func (l LogReader) Read(p []byte) (int, error) {
    // custom Read — the OVERRIDDEN method
    // l.Reader.Read still available for the forward
}
```

Methods you don't redeclare forward automatically; methods you do redeclare shadow the promotion. One mechanism, exactly the decorator pattern: embed the interface, override what you wrap, inherit the forwarding of everything else.

The standard library's exhibits: bufio.Reader (embeds io.Reader — every bufio method that ISN'T Read is declared, and the embedded reader is the forward target), sort.Reverse (embeds sort.Interface, overrides ONLY Less — the three-line decorator), and the middleware pattern every Go web framework uses: handler struct { next http.Handler } IS this pattern with a different field spelling — embedding http.Handler promotes ServeHTTP, and overriding it IS middleware.

Why interface embedding rather than a named field: the named field gives NO satisfaction and NO promotion (must hand-write each forwarding method); the embedded interface gives both — the compiler writes the delegation for every method you don't override. That's the entire difference between a five-method forwarding boilerplate and one embedded line.
